From a8f2e41c0d9b3e1f5c7a02d9e6b4f8c1a3d5e7f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cole Walker <cole.walker@windriver.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Jul 2026 14:15:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] port: Fix master loss detection on inhibit_announce ports

On unicast T-BC slave ports configured with inhibit_announce=1, the
announce receipt timeout handler clears the timer permanently and
preserves the foreign master record. When the upstream master dies,
BMCA is signaled but sees the stale record and concludes the master
is still present. The port remains stuck in SLAVE state indefinitely.

Observed: ptsptp2 (unicast T-BC slave, domain 44) stays in SLAVE for
6+ minutes after ptsptp1 (T-GM) is stopped. PMC becomes unresponsive.
No ANNOUNCE_RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_EXPIRES event is ever generated.

Root cause: The inhibit_announce early-return path:
1. Clears the announce timer (port_clr_tmo) - never re-arms
2. Preserves the foreign master record - BMCA thinks master is alive
3. Returns EV_NONE - no FSM state transition

Fix: differentiate behavior by port state:
- SLAVE/UNCALIBRATED: the master SHOULD be actively sending. If the
  timeout fires, the master is genuinely gone. Clear the foreign
  master record so BMCA can react, re-arm the timer, and return
  EV_ANNOUNCE_RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_EXPIRES to trigger a state transition.
- LISTENING/PASSIVE: preserve the existing behavior (keep record
  intact, signal BMCA only). These states need the record preserved
  for initial grant negotiation and startup convergence.

This also fixes the secondary symptom where delay_req floods to the
dead master address saturate the poll loop - once BMCA transitions
the port out of SLAVE, delay requests stop.

Signed-off-by: Cole Walker <cole.walker@windriver.com>
---
 port.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/port.c b/port.c
index 147e0ad..c3a8b9f 100644
--- a/port.c
+++ b/port.c
@@ -2911,14 +2911,27 @@
 
 		if (p->inhibit_announce) {
 			/*
-			 * inhibit_announce ports cannot re-announce, so they
-			 * cannot rebuild a cleared foreign master record.
-			 * Preserve the record and signal BMCA to re-evaluate.
+			 * In SLAVE/UNCALIBRATED: the master should be actively
+			 * sending. Timeout means it is gone - clear the record
+			 * and fire the timeout event so the FSM can transition.
+			 *
+			 * In LISTENING/PASSIVE: preserve the foreign master
+			 * record (cannot be rebuilt without re-announcing) and
+			 * signal BMCA to re-evaluate without a state transition.
 			 */
-			port_clr_tmo(p->fda.fd[FD_ANNOUNCE_TIMER]);
-			clock_set_sde(p->clock, 1);
-			delay_req_prune(p);
-			return EV_NONE;
+			if (p->state == PS_SLAVE || p->state == PS_UNCALIBRATED) {
+				if (p->best) {
+					fc_clear(p->best);
+				}
+				port_set_announce_tmo(p);
+				delay_req_prune(p);
+				return EV_ANNOUNCE_RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_EXPIRES;
+			} else {
+				port_clr_tmo(p->fda.fd[FD_ANNOUNCE_TIMER]);
+				clock_set_sde(p->clock, 1);
+				delay_req_prune(p);
+				return EV_NONE;
+			}
 		}
 
 		/* Normal ports: clear the foreign master record on timeout */
-- 
